00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:48.190
they degenerate into a combination of people asking random questions in the thin air, people prognosticating, people politicking, people bickering, people talking about stuff that has not germane to the work. And they become largely entertainment platforms for group culture building, which is fine, with a high noise to signal ratio. Whereas if you don't have Slack, if you have a question, you have to really think about and try to solve it yourself. And if you can't, you have to figure out who in the company might have an answer to that question.

